data.reasoningText:
Now I have a good picture of the project. Let me plan the components and then implement them all.

## Component Plan: Team Messaging Chat

### Component Tree
```
ChatPage                    (page — owns all state, orchestrates layout)
├── ChannelSidebar          (channel list, active selection)
│   └── ChannelItem         (single channel row — name, desc, badge)
├── MessageArea             (main message panel)
│   ├── MessageItem         (single message with reactions, hover actions)
│   └── MessageInput        (textarea + send button, reused in thread)
└── ThreadPanel             (collapsible right panel)
```

I'll structure the data flow so ChatPage manages all the state—channels, messages, the selected channel, and thread data—then pass down the relevant pieces and callbacks to each child component. The implementation order starts with the models and service layer, then builds up from the leaf components like ChannelItem before assembling the larger containers.

For file organization, I'm putting the service interface and models in Services/IChatService.cs, then creating a Components/Pages/Chat/ folder to house ChatPage.razor as the main page, with ChannelSidebar, ChannelItem, MessageArea, and ThreadPanel as child components, plus a shared MessageInput component for reuse.

```

csharp
public interface IChatService
{
    Task<List<Channel>> GetChannelsAsync();
    Task<List<Message>> GetMessagesAsync(string channelId);
    Task SendMessageAsync(string channelId, string content);
    Task<List<Message>> GetThreadMessagesAsync(string messageId);
}

public record Channel(string Id, string Name, string Description);
public record Message(string Id, string ChannelId, string Content, string Author, DateTime Timestamp, List<Reaction> Reactions);
public record Reaction(string Emoji, List<string> Users);
```

Now I'm thinking about the layout strategy — the chat page needs full-height coverage, so I'll either create a dedicated ChatLayout or override the MainLayout styles within the chat page itself. The cleaner approach is probably a minimal ChatLayout that removes the default sidebar and top navigation, letting the chat components handle their own layout.

Let me start building the component files now, beginning with the channel sidebar and message display components.

